window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-2-full-book"][39] = "<h1>JOURNEY 4: VEGA — Internal AI Eval Platform at a Mid-Stage Fintech</h1>\n<h2>23.4.1 Origin and context</h2>\n<p>This journey is different from the previous three — it's an <strong>internal product</strong>. The PM is not building for external customers; they're building for engineering and ML teams inside their own company.</p>\n<p>Internal products are often more interesting than external ones because the dynamics are different:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your customers are colleagues. They can't churn easily; they also have political weight.</li>\n<li>Your &quot;competitors&quot; are: build-it-yourself, buy from vendor (e.g., LangSmith, Braintrust, Arize), or do nothing.</li>\n<li>ROI is measured in engineering productivity and risk reduction, not revenue.</li>\n<li>Failure is silent. People just stop using your tool, but they won't say it directly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The context: a mid-stage Indian fintech (~₹500Cr revenue, ~150 engineers, ~25 ML/AI engineers). Over the past 18 months, the company has shipped 12 LLM-powered features — fraud detection summaries, customer support copilot, document parsing for KYC, transaction descriptions, etc. Each feature has its own ad-hoc evaluation: a Jupyter notebook, a manual sample, a custom dashboard.</p>\n<p>The pattern is unsustainable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No one knows which feature's eval is current vs stale.</li>\n<li>Different teams use different metrics; comparisons are impossible.</li>\n<li>Regression detection is reactive (a customer complaint triggers investigation).</li>\n<li>Onboarding new ML engineers requires re-explaining eval setup every time.</li>\n<li>Compliance/risk asks: &quot;Show us the eval coverage of every LLM-touching feature.&quot; Nobody can answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hypothesis: a unified internal evaluation platform — Vega — that provides a standard way to define, run, and monitor AI evals across the company would meaningfully improve AI feature quality and reduce risk.</p>\n<h2>23.4.2 Discovery — internal interviews</h2>\n<p>For internal products, &quot;discovery&quot; is interviewing colleagues. Twenty interviews:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>10 ML/AI engineers building LLM features.</li>\n<li>4 engineering managers.</li>\n<li>3 from risk/compliance.</li>\n<li>2 from product (PMs of AI features).</li>\n<li>1 CTO.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What we asked:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Walk me through how you currently evaluate the LLM feature you're working on.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;When was the last time you caught a regression before it shipped? When was the last time you missed one?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;If a regulator asked tomorrow how you ensure your AI features are safe, how would you answer?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;What's the most painful part of your eval workflow?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What we learned:</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 1: Eval set fragmentation.</strong> &quot;Every feature has its own eval set. Some have 50 examples; some have 500. Some are versioned; some aren't. Some are in Jupyter; some in Notion.&quot; (8 of 10 engineers.)</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 2: No regression detection.</strong> &quot;We added a new prompt last month. We didn't run the old eval set against the new prompt because that would take half a day. Two weeks later, support got complaints about a regression we'd introduced.&quot; (5 of 10 engineers — and these are people willing to admit it.)</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 3: No production monitoring.</strong> &quot;We track latency and error rate in production. We don't track quality. We have no idea if the model started hallucinating last Tuesday.&quot; (9 of 10 engineers.)</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 4: Cross-team blindness.</strong> EMs: &quot;I have 5 engineers shipping LLM features. I don't know which has good evals and which has none. I'm flying blind on quality.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 5: Compliance anxiety.</strong> Risk/compliance: &quot;Regulators are starting to ask about AI governance. Right now we have a few PowerPoint slides. We'd be in trouble if there's an incident.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Pain 6: Onboarding cost.</strong> &quot;Every new ML engineer takes 2-3 weeks to set up their eval workflow because there's no shared infrastructure. That's tax we keep paying.&quot;</p>\n<h3>What we didn't expect</h3>\n<p>Several engineers expressed a <em>preference</em> for the status quo:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;I have my eval the way I like it. A platform would impose constraints.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;I don't want my evals visible to other teams. If someone sees a 75% pass rate, they'll think my feature is bad.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;I don't want compliance breathing down my neck.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is the platform-PM problem. <strong>Internal users often resist standardization, especially when it brings visibility.</strong> Vega will face active resistance from some users, even though leadership wants the platform. Navigating this is the central PM challenge.</p>\n<h3>JTBD synthesis</h3>\n<p>Two JTBDs, in tension:</p>\n<p><strong>ML engineer JTBD:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>&quot;When I'm shipping or maintaining an AI feature, I want fast eval feedback so I know if my changes improved or regressed quality — without a heavy infrastructure burden, and without exposing my work to unwanted scrutiny.&quot;</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Leadership/compliance JTBD:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>&quot;When I'm responsible for AI quality and risk across the company, I want unified visibility into eval coverage, regression detection, and quality trends — so I can answer 'are we safe?' with evidence.&quot;</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>These two jobs sometimes align (good evals serve both) and sometimes conflict (engineers don't want their numbers visible). The PM has to design for both, with explicit visibility controls.</p>\n<h2>23.4.3 The PRD (compressed)</h2>\n<p><strong>Product:</strong> Vega — Internal AI evaluation platform.</p>\n<p><strong>Core capabilities:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Eval set management.</strong> Versioned eval sets stored centrally. CSV/JSONL upload, programmatic creation, organic growth from production logs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Eval runner.</strong> Run any eval set against any LLM/prompt configuration. Cached results. Compare runs side-by-side.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>LLM-as-judge framework.</strong> Out-of-the-box judges for common dimensions (correctness, toxicity, format adherence). Custom judges easy to create.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>CI/CD integration.</strong> Plug into existing GitHub Actions / Jenkins. Eval runs are part of the deploy pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Production monitoring.</strong> Sample production traffic; run shadow evals; alert on quality drops.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dashboards and reporting.</strong> Per-feature views, cross-feature comparisons, executive summary.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Visibility controls.</strong> Eval results visible to: feature team only / engineering org / leadership. Configurable. Engineers control their visibility.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Primary users:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tier 1: ML/AI engineers (most direct usage).</li>\n<li>Tier 2: EMs of teams shipping AI features.</li>\n<li>Tier 3: Risk/compliance, leadership.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>&quot;Pricing&quot;:</strong> No external pricing. Internal cost: 1 PM, 4 engineers, 1 designer, ~12 months to v1. Approximate cost ~₹5Cr fully loaded.</p>\n<p><strong>Success metrics:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adoption: % of LLM-touching features in the company using Vega.</li>\n<li>Eval coverage: % of production LLM calls with active eval coverage.</li>\n<li>Time to detect regression (MTTD): from change deployed to regression flagged.</li>\n<li>Engineer satisfaction (NPS among engineers using Vega).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-goals:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>We are not building model training infrastructure.</li>\n<li>We are not building inference infrastructure (that's another team's domain).</li>\n<li>We are not building a product analytics platform (Mixpanel etc. own that).</li>\n<li>We are not handling RLHF / fine-tuning data labeling at MVP.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>23.4.4 Architecture decisions</h2>\n<h3>Build vs Buy vs Hybrid</h3>\n<p>The hardest strategic question for an internal platform.</p>\n<p><strong>Buy options (in 2026):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>LangSmith (LangChain's offering).</li>\n<li>Braintrust.</li>\n<li>Arize Phoenix.</li>\n<li>Weights &amp; Biases (Weave).</li>\n<li>Helicone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Build option:</strong> internal platform from scratch.</p>\n<p><strong>Hybrid:</strong> integrate one of the above as the engine; build internal layer for our specifics.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision: Hybrid, leaning toward Braintrust as engine + internal wrapper.</strong></p>\n<p>Reasoning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Building eval infrastructure from scratch is 12-18 months of work. Most of it is undifferentiated (eval runner, score storage, dashboard).</li>\n<li>Vendors do this part well. They've solved problems we'd take years to solve.</li>\n<li>The differentiating layer is: integration with our internal systems (CI/CD, monitoring, compliance reporting), our specific eval patterns, our org-specific UX and governance.</li>\n<li>Braintrust specifically: strong API, good multi-tenant model, reasonable pricing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trade-off accepted:</strong> vendor lock-in. If Braintrust changes pricing, sunsets a feature, or gets acquired, we have to migrate. Mitigation: keep our wrapper layer abstract; design for substitutability.</p>\n<h3>LLM-as-judge architecture</h3>\n<p>Most evaluations in 2026 use LLM-as-judge for subjective dimensions (quality, helpfulness, tone). The architecture:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Judge LLM (typically a frontier model — GPT-4o, Claude Sonnet) scores outputs on each dimension.</li>\n<li>Multiple judges available; engineers choose based on need.</li>\n<li>Calibration: 5-10% of judge outputs randomly sampled for human review; judge prompt revised when human-judge disagreement is high.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The risk with LLM-as-judge: drift. The judge model itself updates; scores shift; what looks like a regression might just be the judge changing its mind.</p>\n<p>Mitigation: pin judge model versions; rotate carefully; track judge consistency over time.</p>\n<h3>Production monitoring</h3>\n<p>Three patterns:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Shadow mode.</strong> Run new configurations against production traffic without serving the result. Compare quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Canary mode.</strong> Serve new configuration to 5% of traffic; compare quality with control.</li>\n<li><strong>Full rollout monitoring.</strong> After 100% rollout, continuously sample and run quality checks.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>All three have value at different lifecycle stages. Vega supports all.</p>\n<h3>Storage and data architecture</h3>\n<p>Eval data is sensitive. Specific decisions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eval results stored in our data warehouse (Snowflake), not Braintrust's infrastructure.</li>\n<li>Eval inputs/outputs: encrypted at rest. Access-controlled by team.</li>\n<li>Production samples: redacted of PII before being stored as eval data.</li>\n<li>Audit log for all eval data access (compliance requirement).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>23.4.5 The metrics tree</h2>\n<pre><code>                  Vega adoption (% of LLM features using)\n                                |\n            ____________________|____________________\n           |                                         |\n       Active feature        Engineer satisfaction\n       teams using Vega      with Vega\n           |                                         |\n       _____|_____                          _________|________\n      |           |                        |                  |\n   First-time   Returning              Setup time      Regression\n   feature      feature use            (minutes from   detection\n   onboarding   (eval runs/            zero to first   value (MTTD\n   completion   week per team)         eval result)    improvement)\n      |\n   Time-to-\n   first-eval\n   (from team\n   joining to\n   first run)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The leading indicator we watch most: <strong>time-to-first-eval for new feature teams</strong>. If it takes &gt;2 hours from &quot;I want to use Vega&quot; to &quot;first eval result,&quot; teams give up. Target: &lt;30 minutes.</p>\n<p>The killer adoption signal: feature teams <em>recruiting</em> other teams to use Vega. When that happens, we've crossed the chasm internally.</p>\n<h2>23.4.6 The MVP plan</h2>\n<p><strong>Phase 1 (months 1-4): Core platform</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eval set management.</li>\n<li>Eval runner with Braintrust integration.</li>\n<li>2-3 judge templates.</li>\n<li>Per-feature dashboards.</li>\n<li>3 design-partner teams (the most willing internal customers).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Phase 2 (months 5-8): Production monitoring + CI/CD</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Production sampling.</li>\n<li>Shadow mode.</li>\n<li>GitHub Actions integration.</li>\n<li>8-12 teams using.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Phase 3 (months 9-12): Compliance + scale</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Org-wide dashboards for leadership/compliance.</li>\n<li>Cross-team comparisons (carefully, with visibility controls).</li>\n<li>Self-serve onboarding for any team.</li>\n<li>80%+ adoption target.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Kill criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Below 50% adoption among AI-shipping teams by month 12: kill or radical rethink (perhaps deprecate in favor of pure vendor).</li>\n<li>Engineer NPS below 0 (more detractors than promoters) consistently: kill or rebuild.</li>\n<li>Regression-detection MTTD doesn't improve materially: tool isn't doing its job.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>23.4.7 Trade-offs accepted</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hybrid (vendor + wrapper) &gt; pure build or pure buy.</strong> Faster ship; some lock-in.</li>\n<li><strong>Engineer-controlled visibility &gt; leadership-driven visibility.</strong> Engineers will adopt; leadership gets the dashboards they need eventually, not on day one. Sequence matters.</li>\n<li><strong>Pull &gt; push for adoption.</strong> No mandate from CTO that teams must use Vega. Adoption earned. Slower; durable.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-service &gt; white-glove for v1.</strong> Some teams will struggle; we accept that to focus on the path-to-scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Templates &gt; full customization at MVP.</strong> Limited eval template options; expand once we know what teams need.</li>\n<li><strong>Production sampling &gt; live evaluation.</strong> Sampling is cheaper, sufficient for monitoring; live evaluation is too expensive at scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Plain dashboards &gt; beautiful design at MVP.</strong> Internal product; engineers tolerate ugly; we save design budget for the parts that matter.</li>\n<li><strong>Engineering-first &gt; product-first language.</strong> UX speaks engineering language (PRs, configs, metrics). Less friction for the target user; less accessible to non-engineers; intentional.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>23.4.8 Post-launch retrospective</h2>\n<p><strong>Year 1, looking back:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What worked:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hybrid architecture was right. We shipped MVP in 4 months instead of 12 by leveraging Braintrust.</li>\n<li>Design-partner teams generated genuine word-of-mouth. By month 6, teams were asking us for access; we didn't have to recruit.</li>\n<li>CI/CD integration was sticky. Once a team set up GitHub Actions to run evals on PRs, removing it was harder than keeping it.</li>\n<li>Production monitoring caught 3 real regressions in the first 6 months that would have shipped to customers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What didn't:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adoption stalled at ~55% by month 12. Some teams resisted (especially older teams with established workflows). Cultural change takes time.</li>\n<li>Cross-team comparison dashboards launched in Phase 3 caused an uproar — some teams felt exposed. We made comparison opt-in only.</li>\n<li>Compliance reporting was harder than expected. Each compliance question had nuances we didn't anticipate. Required custom reports per request.</li>\n<li>LLM-as-judge drift was real. Judge model updates caused score shifts that looked like regressions but weren't. Took 3 months of investigation to recognize the pattern.</li>\n<li>Some teams gamed evals — added easy questions to their eval set to inflate pass rates. We added &quot;eval set diversity&quot; metrics; partially solved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What we changed in v1.x:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Made cross-team dashboards opt-in. Restored team trust.</li>\n<li>Built &quot;judge model snapshot&quot; feature — pin judge to specific version per eval set.</li>\n<li>Started weekly office hours for teams; helped with adoption.</li>\n<li>Built self-serve &quot;starter eval set&quot; for common feature types.</li>\n<li>Added eval set diversity scoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What we'd do differently:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Engagement strategy with non-believer teams sooner. We focused on the willing teams; should have invested in winning over the skeptical ones earlier.</li>\n<li>Compliance partnership earlier. Should have had compliance team co-design the dashboards; would have saved rework.</li>\n<li>Judge model versioning from day one. Lesson learned from production drift.</li>\n<li>More explicit &quot;this won't be visible to others&quot; messaging early. Trust mattered more than we expected.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>23.4.9 What would Google look for here?</h2>\n<p>Internal-platform PM is a different beast. Strong signals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Awareness of the build-buy-hybrid tradeoff.</strong> This is a senior PM signal.</li>\n<li><strong>User segmentation across multiple stakeholder layers.</strong> Engineers vs EMs vs leadership vs compliance — all customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Trust-aware design.</strong> The visibility-control discussion shows political awareness.</li>\n<li><strong>Adoption-led metrics.</strong> Pull &gt; push; word-of-mouth as adoption measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Honest about resistance.</strong> &quot;Some teams resisted&quot; is a real platform-PM truth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Likely follow-ups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Some teams are still resisting at month 12. What's your strategy to win them over?&quot; (Influence under resistance.)</li>\n<li>&quot;If a senior leader mandates Vega usage, do you support that?&quot; (Tests philosophy on top-down adoption.)</li>\n<li>&quot;How do you decide when to deprecate features that aren't gaining traction?&quot; (Platform discipline.)</li>\n<li>&quot;Walk me through how you'd think about open-sourcing parts of Vega.&quot; (Strategic thinking on internal-vs-external products.)</li>\n<li>&quot;What's the long-term position of internal AI tooling vs vendor solutions?&quot; (Build-vs-buy strategic horizon.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This kind of internal-platform thinking is rare among early-career PMs. If you can speak fluently to it, you signal senior-grade thinking.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>End of Journey 4.</em></p>\n<p>\\newpage</p>\n";
